nodes.goal:
Enumerate the open items in the work queue: {run.work_queue}
Read them with whatever read-only tools you have. Ignore items closed/answered out-of-band. Return up to 20 items, each reduced to one line.
Reply with ONLY fenced json {"items": [{"id": "<stable identifier>", "summary": "<one line>"}], "count": <int>, "notes": "<anything unreadable or skipped, with reason>"}

Hand the routine set to the batch lanes. From the classify payload (## Inputs): if has_urgent is true, reply {"routine": [], "skipped": "urgent arm active — belt on the prune law"} and do nothing else.
Otherwise re-emit the routine array VERBATIM (no re-classifying, no additions): {"routine": [<each {id, action} exactly as classify wrote it>], "count": <int>}
This node exists because the fan-out grammar requires its items_from head in the lane's own `after` — and an `after` edge to classify would resurrect this arm while the human holds the urgent gate (prune law: all deps must be skipped; a live classifier dep keeps the arm alive).

The human answered the escalation gate with an option and reason (see ## Inputs from 'escalate'). If the released option is NOT 'act', do nothing to the items: reply {"acted": [], "skipped": "not approved: <option>"} and stop.
If it IS 'act': execute exactly and only what the human's release reason AUTHORIZED (named in the release text) for the urgent items (in the classify payload above / gate context). If the release says bare 'act' with no concrete authorization, execute nothing: reply {"acted": [], "skipped": "'act' carried no authorization — owner must name what to do"} and surface it. Record per item what was done and the evidence.
Reply with ONLY fenced json {"acted": [{"id": "<id>", "action": "<what was done>", "evidence": "<command/output>"}], "skipped": "<reason or empty>"}

Execute the rule's prescribed action for EACH routine item in the classify payload (## Inputs).
Apply actions at the action target: {run.action_target}
If the urgent arm is visible in inputs (has_urgent=true), do nothing and reply {"items": [], "skipped": "urgent arm active"} — belt-and-braces on the prune law.
Work items one by one; an item whose target row cannot be located gets action_taken 'not applied: <why>' — never invent a target or write anywhere else.
Reply with ONLY fenced json {"items": [{"id": "<item id>", "action_taken": "<what was done, or 'not applied: <why>'>", "evidence": "<command/output establishing it>"}], "count": <int>}

fanout.goal:
Execute the rule's prescribed action for this routine item: {item}
Apply it at the action target: {run.action_target}
Work only this item. If the item's target row cannot be located in the action target, do NOT invent a target or write anywhere else: report action_taken as 'not applied: <why>' with your probe evidence.
Reply with ONLY fenced json {"id": "<item id>", "action_taken": "<what was done, or 'not applied: <why>'>", "evidence": "<command/output establishing it>"}
